WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.460
In 1953 a surgeon removed tissue from the inner part of both temporal lobes of a

2
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.780
young man. He had severe epilepsy.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.660
Afterwards, the patient known as H.M.

4
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.440
could not remember events from an hour earlier.

5
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.100
Yet he could still learn a new hand skill.

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.760
Is memory one ability, or several?

7
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:24.203
The test is a dissociation.

8
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:28.163
One ability fails after damage, while another survives.

9
00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:30.683
H.M.'s digit span was normal.

10
00:00:31.123 --> 00:00:33.543
That is how many digits he could repeat back at once.

11
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:36.883
He could hold a number as long as he kept rehearsing it.

12
00:00:37.163 --> 00:00:38.683
So working memory worked.

13
00:00:39.203 --> 00:00:43.323
But almost nothing on that notepad reached long -term declarative memory.

14
00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:45.703
His skill learning survived too.

15
00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:50.703
One task fails, another survives: a single dissociation.

16
00:00:51.323 --> 00:00:53.563
It could mean only that the task is harder.

17
00:00:54.103 --> 00:00:58.023
So take a worked problem, with invented numbers, in the design of the Knowlton

18
00:00:58.023 --> 00:00:59.923
study. There are two tasks.

19
00:01:00.423 --> 00:01:03.943
A two -choice classification, where chance is 50%.

20
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:08.983
And a four -choice questionnaire about the training, where chance is 25%.

21
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:11.393
Controls score 70 and 80.

22
00:01:11.893 --> 00:01:14.053
Amnesic patients score 68 and 40.

23
00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:17.333
Parkinson's patients score 52 and 78.

24
00:01:17.853 --> 00:01:19.093
Look at 40 against 80.

25
00:01:19.553 --> 00:01:20.713
It looks like half of normal.

26
00:01:21.093 --> 00:01:22.733
But raw scores hide the guessing.

27
00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:25.557
So subtract chance first.

28
00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:28.397
Then divide by the controls' gain above chance.

29
00:01:28.937 --> 00:01:33.077
The amnesic questionnaire gives 15 over 55, which is 27%.

30
00:01:33.077 --> 00:01:38.117
percent. That is about a quarter, not a half, because a quarter of answers are right

31
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:41.317
by guessing. Amnesic classification gives 90%.

32
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:45.097
percent, Parkinson's patients get 10%, percent, and 96%.

33
00:01:45.097 --> 00:01:46.097
percent.

34
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.140
Now compare. Each group fails the task the other passes.

35
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.040
That is a double dissociation.

36
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.100
No single difficulty scale fits, because the task that is hard for one group is easy

37
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.740
for the other. It supports two separable learning processes.

38
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.460
It does not pin each to one structure, because Parkinson's disease damages more than

39
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.340
the striatum. A lesion shows that a structure is needed, not that the memory lives

40
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:12.660
there.
